WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.960
The first film turned molecular events into reads.

2
00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:06.580
How do millions of reads become one genome?

3
00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:09.580
Some overlap unique places.

4
00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:12.240
Others seem to fit several.

5
00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:17.880
The computer must separate missing evidence from evidence that cannot be placed.

6
00:00:21.417 --> 00:00:22.637
Start with sampling.

7
00:00:24.317 --> 00:00:27.397
Coverage is the mean number of reads lying over each base.

8
00:00:28.777 --> 00:00:31.797
As more gold reads land at random, blue gaps shrink.

9
00:00:33.057 --> 00:00:35.597
The mean does not promise equal stacks everywhere.

10
00:00:36.937 --> 00:00:40.417
A position can still receive many reads, few reads, or none.

11
00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:47.140
Under ideal random sampling, the unread fraction falls exponentially as mean

12
00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:52.280
coverage rises. Adding reads therefore attacks a sampling problem.

13
00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:58.160
It makes an unseen position less likely, although real libraries are less even.

14
00:00:59.233 --> 00:01:03.853
Now the same red repeat block appears at two places along the blue ribbon.

15
00:01:05.133 --> 00:01:09.353
A short gold read from inside that block matches both places equally.

16
00:01:10.713 --> 00:01:14.153
Its bases may be accurate, yet its address is ambiguous.

17
00:01:15.673 --> 00:01:19.693
More copies of that same short evidence do not create a unique address.

18
00:01:20.783 --> 00:01:24.523
Another pile of short reads is ready to land on the repeats.

19
00:01:25.923 --> 00:01:29.203
Will a deeper pile tell which flanking regions belong together?

20
00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:38.510
The pile grows, but both placements remain possible.

21
00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:43.830
More reads fill sampling gaps; reads that span a repeat place it.

22
00:01:45.070 --> 00:01:50.170
Now one long gold read crosses the red repeat and reaches blue unique sequence on

23
00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:54.530
both sides. That span links the repeat copy to its flanks.

24
00:01:55.583 --> 00:02:01.383
De novo assembly joins reads without a reference, first producing contigs or an

25
00:02:01.383 --> 00:02:07.283
assembly graph. Reference alignment asks where reads fit on a reference path or

26
00:02:07.283 --> 00:02:08.483
graph already chosen.

27
00:02:09.783 --> 00:02:11.183
Now the reference branches.

28
00:02:12.463 --> 00:02:14.963
A read missing from one path can fit another.

29
00:02:16.263 --> 00:02:19.583
What counts as a reported difference changes with the reference.

30
00:02:20.717 --> 00:02:26.097
Long accurate reads and ultra-long spans closed difficult gaps in one human

31
00:02:26.097 --> 00:02:32.177
assembly. A pangenome then added alternative paths from many chromosome copies.

32
00:02:33.657 --> 00:02:35.917
Completeness asks whether sequence is missing.

33
00:02:37.257 --> 00:02:40.857
Representation asks whether one path captures human variation.

34
00:02:42.067 --> 00:02:44.367
Reference choice changes two stages.

35
00:02:45.807 --> 00:02:49.107
First, it changes whether a read has a close place to align.

36
00:02:50.347 --> 00:02:53.607
Then it changes which base or segment is labelled different.

37
00:02:54.807 --> 00:03:00.087
A variant call is therefore a comparison, not a claim about rarity, selection, or

38
00:03:00.087 --> 00:03:01.087
health.

39
00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:05.760
Genome results depend on what each read records and where it fits.

40
00:03:07.060 --> 00:03:10.240
Was the region sampled, and was the evidence placed uniquely?

41
00:03:11.740 --> 00:03:13.100
Extra reads help the first.

42
00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:16.560
Reads that span the repeat help the second.

43
00:03:17.740 --> 00:03:21.780
A richer reference gives reads from missing sequence a place to land.

44
00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:25.820
Those are different limits, and they need different fixes.
